{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"08","chapter_title":"GitHub Pages Personal Website","title":"Module 6 Website Studio","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting module 6 website studio to GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website","cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/06/07"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_calibration"]},"content":"# Module 6 Website Studio\n\n![Module 6 Website Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-08-module-6-website-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThis is the capstone where the seven prior sections become one published site. Until now you practiced each skill in isolation: architecture, markup, repo setup, publishing, debugging, evidence, maintenance. The studio asks you to integrate them and prove the result works end to end. Integration is its own skill, because a site can pass each check individually and still fail as a whole, for example a perfectly accessible page that 404s on the live URL, or a clean file tree with a secret quietly sitting in history.\n\nThe work product here is a single, deliberate verification: assemble the publishable file tree, run one combined accessibility, privacy, and publishing checklist, attach an honest AI-use note, and confirm everything on the live URL the way a visitor would. The deliverable is not a mockup or a description; it is a working, inspectable, published site.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student believes she is done. Her About page is accessible, her repo is clean, her resume bullet is sharp, her CSS is tidy. She submits a folder of files. But she never enabled Pages on the right branch, so the site is not actually live; she never opened the published URL on a phone, so she did not notice the project links 404 from the subpath; and although she deleted a stray token file, it still sits in commit history. Each individual skill from the module is present, yet the submission fails as a published artifact because nothing tied the pieces together and tested them as a system.\n\nThe studio exists to prevent exactly this. Passing every section separately is necessary but not sufficient; the capstone is about the integration and the single end-to-end proof.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nIntegration in this studio means three things working together.\n\n**One file tree that satisfies every prior section at once.** The tree must reflect the architecture decision (only pages that serve the purpose), the markup decision (valid HTML with alt text), the repo decision (a `.gitignore`, no secrets), and the publishing decision (lowercase `index.html`, relative asset paths). The tree is the place where all the module's choices become simultaneously visible.\n\n**One combined checklist across three dimensions.** Rather than three separate passes, the studio runs accessibility, privacy, and publishing as a single review where each check names its evidence in the artifact. Accessibility: every informative image has descriptive alt, navigation uses real links. Privacy: no keys, address, or phone committed, with `.gitignore` in place. Publishing: Pages source set, lowercase entry file, relative paths. The discipline is that each row points to something concrete in the site, not to a claim that it is fine.\n\n**One end-to-end verification on the live URL.** The decisive test is a single visit to the published address in a private window on a phone. That one action exercises accessibility (the screen reader announces alt text), privacy (no secrets surface), and publishing (pages load, links resolve, layout holds) at the same time. A live test that passes is worth more than any number of local previews, because it is the environment your audience actually uses.\n\nAll three course anchors converge here. NACE career readiness treats shipping a working public artifact as the real outcome. ISTE digital citizenship covers the privacy and accessibility obligations of anything you publish. data-ink and visual evidence applies to your proof: the strongest evidence is the live site itself, demonstrated, not asserted.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Every section passed in isolation: accessible markup, clean latest snapshot, sharp resume bullet. You submit a folder of files. Is the studio complete?\" explanation=\"Passing each section separately is necessary but not sufficient. A site can be accessible yet 404 from the live subpath, and a clean latest snapshot can still hide a secret in history. The studio requires one integrated end-to-end test on the published URL, not a folder of correct files.\" options='[{\"text\":\"No: you must verify the integrated result on the live URL, since sections can pass alone yet fail as a whole\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes: if each section passed, the assembled site is guaranteed to work\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Yes, as long as the folder of files is complete and well organized\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThis studio integrates the whole module. Maria assembles the final file tree, runs the three-part checklist, and verifies on the live URL.\n\n**Step 1. Confirm the publishable file tree.**\n\n```text\nmlopez.github.io/\n  index.html        (Home, links to all pages)\n  about.html        (bio + headshot)\n  projects/\n    index.html      (project cards)\n    dashboard.html\n  resume.pdf\n  assets/\n    style.css\n  .gitignore        (token.txt, .DS_Store)\n```\n\n**Step 2. Run the accessibility, privacy, publishing checklist.**\n\n| Check | Evidence in the artifact |\n| --- | --- |\n| Accessibility | Every <img> has descriptive alt; nav uses real <a> links |\n| Privacy | No API keys, address, or phone committed; .gitignore in place |\n| Publishing | Settings, Pages source set; lowercase index.html; relative asset paths |\n\n**Step 3. Add the AI-use note.** \"AI assisted with drafting starter HTML, debugging one unclosed tag, and rewording one resume bullet. I verified every change in the browser and own all factual claims.\"\n\n**Step 4. Verify end to end like a visitor.** Open https://mlopez.github.io in a private window on a phone: pages load, the headshot announces its alt text, all nav and project links resolve, and no secrets appear in the repo.\n\nThe submission is now a real published site, not a mockup. One end-to-end test on the live URL exercises accessibility, privacy, and publishing together, and the AI-use note keeps the work honest and auditable.\n\nOrder the capstone integration steps so the single live-URL test comes last and subsumes the rest. Assemble and check the pieces first, then prove the whole on the real address.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Assemble one file tree satisfying architecture, markup, repo hygiene, and publishing\",\"Run the combined checklist, naming concrete evidence for accessibility, privacy, and publishing\",\"Confirm the whole commit history is clean, not just the latest snapshot\",\"Attach an honest AI-use note covering every tool-assisted change\",\"Open the live URL in a private window on a phone as the one end-to-end test\",\"Confirm pages load, alt text announces, links resolve, and no secret surfaces\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you call the studio complete, the submission must pass one integrated test, not three separate ones:\n\n1. **Tree check:** Does the file tree simultaneously satisfy architecture, markup, repo hygiene, and publishing requirements? Any one missing breaks the whole.\n2. **Live check:** Open the published URL in a private window on a phone. Do pages load, do all links resolve, does alt text announce, does the layout hold?\n3. **History check:** Is the repo clean across its whole history, not just the latest snapshot? A secret in an old commit still fails privacy.\n\nIf the live check passes on the real URL, you have a published site. If you only verified locally, you have not finished the studio.\n\nWalk a submission that passed every section in isolation through the integrated checks and predict each verdict before you reveal it. It was never tested on the live URL.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the tree, live, and history checks on the never-tested submission\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Tree check first: the file tree has purposeful pages, valid markup, a .gitignore, and a lowercase index.html. Does the tree satisfy all four dimensions at once?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, the tree itself looks complete across architecture, markup, repo, and publishing\",\"No, the tree cannot be trusted until the site is tested live\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"The tree does pass on paper: it shows architecture, markup, repo hygiene, and publishing readiness together. But a passing tree is necessary, not sufficient.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Live check next: the project links were never opened on the live subpath. What does opening the published URL on a phone reveal?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Nothing new, since the local preview already looked right\",\"The project links 404 from the subpath because the paths are absolute\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The live subpath exposes absolute paths that work locally but 404 online. Only the real URL surfaces this, which is why the live check is decisive.\"},{\"reveal\":\"History check: a deleted token still sits in an old commit, so privacy fails across history even though the latest snapshot looks clean. Verdict: incomplete. Fix the paths, rotate and purge the secret, and re-verify on the live URL before submitting.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe central misreading at this stage is \"I passed every section, so the site is done.\" Sections passed in isolation can still combine into a broken whole: accessible markup served from a path that 404s, or a tidy current snapshot hiding a leaked key in history. Integration must be tested as integration.\n\nA second misreading is submitting files instead of a live site. A folder of correct files is not a published artifact. The capstone is specifically about the deployed, verifiable result, which is why the live-URL test is non-negotiable.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rule to a clean capstone. Maria first confirms the publishable file tree: `index.html` at the root, an `about.html` with headshot and alt text, a `projects/` folder, `resume.pdf`, an `assets/style.css`, and a `.gitignore` excluding `token.txt` and `.DS_Store`. The tree alone shows architecture (only purposeful pages), markup (real pages, not placeholders), repo hygiene (the ignore file), and publishing readiness (lowercase index).\n\nShe then runs the combined checklist, naming evidence for each row: accessibility is satisfied because every `<img>` has descriptive alt and the nav uses real `<a>` links; privacy is satisfied because no keys, address, or phone are committed and `.gitignore` is in place; publishing is satisfied because the Pages source is set, the entry file is lowercase, and asset paths are relative. She attaches the AI-use note covering the starter HTML draft, the one debugging fix, and the reworded resume bullet, stating she verified every change and owns all factual claims. Finally she runs the single end-to-end test: opening the live URL in a private window on her phone, where the pages load, the headshot announces its alt text, every link resolves, and no secret appears. One visit exercised all three dimensions at once, which is the point of the studio.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader treats the live visit as the experiment that subsumes the others. As you study the worked example, for each checklist row ask what a visitor on the live URL would actually experience if that row failed (an unannounced image, a leaked key, a 404). If you can map every check to a concrete failure a real visitor would hit, you understand why the integrated live test is the decisive one.\n\nA strong reader of the website studio can answer four questions:\n\n1. How does a single file tree make every prior section's decision visible at once?\n2. Why can a site pass each check separately and still fail as a whole?\n3. What does one live-URL visit on a phone verify that a local preview cannot?\n4. Why is a clean latest snapshot not the same as a clean history?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour capstone is strong when a reviewer can open your live URL, experience an accessible, working, secret-free site, and trace each quality to concrete evidence in the repo. The AI-use note should make your tool use transparent, and the verification should rest on the live site rather than on a claim that it worked locally.\n\nA strong submission names the integration failure you caught: \"Each section passed on its own, but my project links 404ed on the live subpath until I switched to relative paths; I found it only by opening the published URL on my phone, not in the editor.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the integration-and-verify discipline to any capstone deliverable, for example a data report that combines a cleaned spreadsheet, a chart, and a written summary. The components can each be correct while the whole fails: a chart built from an older version of the data, or a summary citing a number the spreadsheet no longer contains. The remedy is the same as in this studio: assemble the pieces into one artifact, run a single combined check that ties each claim to its source, and verify the finished thing in the form the audience will actually receive. Recognizing that \"test the integrated result in its real environment\" is the portable lesson, not \"click the GitHub Pages button,\" is what shows you finished the module rather than just its last task.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-08-module-6-website-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks for CAT 100 06.08. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio","cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"],"stem":"For the Module 6 studio submission, you must deliver a complete published portfolio. Which file tree is ready to publish as a GitHub Pages user site?","options":["A single page.html with all content and no stylesheet or images","index.html, about.html, projects/index.html, resume.pdf, and assets/style.css committed to the username.github.io repo","A Word document exported as portfolio.docx","A folder of screenshots of what the site would look like"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A publishable studio deliverable is the actual file tree (lowercase index.html, linked pages, assets) committed to the correctly named repo, not a mockup or a document.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_mockup_counts_as_published","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio"],"stem":"Your studio checklist covers accessibility, privacy, and publishing. Which combination shows you applied all three module skills?","options":["Bright colors, autoplay music, and a hit counter","Alt text on every image, no API keys or personal data in the repo, and a working live URL verified on another device","A long About page with no images and no links","A single commit message that says done"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The module integrates accessibility (alt text), privacy (no secrets or personal data committed), and publishing (a verified live URL). All three together signal mastery.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_one_skill_stands_in_for_all","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio"],"stem":"You used AI to draft your HTML, debug a CSS issue, and reword a resume bullet. What does the module ask you to include so the work is honest?","options":["Nothing; AI use never needs to be mentioned","A short AI-use note stating where AI helped (drafting, debugging, wording) and confirming you verified the output and own the final decisions","A claim that you wrote everything yourself","The full chat transcript pasted into the home page"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The module's standard is a concise AI-use note that discloses where AI assisted and affirms you checked the output and remain responsible for the result.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_ai_use_left_undisclosed","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_module_6_website_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_6_website_studio","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Before final submission, which single check best confirms the whole module came together for a real visitor?","options":["The repo has many commits","Open the live username.github.io URL in a private window on a phone and confirm pages load, images show alt text, links work, and no personal secrets are exposed","The home page is colorful","You spent a lot of hours on it"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A single end-to-end test on the live URL from another device exercises accessibility, publishing, and privacy at once, which is the integrated outcome the studio targets.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_8_effort_mistaken_for_outcome","difficulty":"medium"}]}}